equation directly,

747
00:41:09,830 --> 00:41:13,095
just straight from the theory,
the step sizes are too small.

748
00:41:13,095 --> 00:41:14,720
Now, when people say
they're too small,

749
00:41:14,720 --> 00:41:17,300
that doesn't mean that there's
anything wrong with them.

750
00:41:17,300 --> 00:41:19,450
It just means it's going
to take way too long.

751
00:41:19,450 --> 00:41:22,270
It just means that
people are impatient,

752
00:41:22,270 --> 00:41:26,390
but they're impatient or were
being very sample inefficient.

753
00:41:26,390 --> 00:41:28,850
So it means that
this is reasonable.

754
00:41:28,850 --> 00:41:29,660
It will hold.

755
00:41:29,660 --> 00:41:31,135
You will get
monotonic improvement.

756
00:41:31,135 --> 00:41:33,260
It's just going to take a
really, really long time.

757
00:41:33,260 --> 00:41:35,320
And it's not going to be
feasible for a lot of things,

758
00:41:35,320 --> 00:41:36,860
or it's not going
to be practical.

759
00:41:36,860 --> 00:41:38,530
And so that is
what sort of helped

760
00:41:38,530 --> 00:41:40,060
motivate why you
might want to tune

761
00:41:40,060 --> 00:41:42,200
the KL penalty, which
we saw last time,

762
00:41:42,200 --> 00:41:44,860
where you sort increase or
decrease how much you care

763
00:41:44,860 --> 00:41:48,220
about this penalty,
or use a trust region,

764
00:41:48,220 --> 00:41:50,240
or use the clipping.

765
00:41:50,240 --> 00:41:52,580
And so that's why we see a
difference between what's

766
00:41:52,580 --> 00:41:55,640
formally guaranteed by if
you were to just directly use

767
00:41:55,640 --> 00:41:59,760
this lower bound versus what's
actually done in practice.

768
00:41:59,760 --> 00:42:02,640
But I think in terms
of the take-homes

769
00:42:02,640 --> 00:42:04,830
from this part on
policy gradient

770
00:42:04,830 --> 00:42:07,110
and PPO is that
it's really useful

771
00:42:07,110 --> 00:42:10,960
to know that you don't just
have to take one gradient step.

772
00:42:10,960 --> 00:42:12,460
You can be much
more data efficient.

773
00:42:12,460 --> 00:42:14,160
You can play this
trick of pretending

774
00:42:14,160 --> 00:42:17,310
there's no change in
the state distribution

775
00:42:17,310 --> 00:42:19,680
in order to take
several gradient steps

776
00:42:19,680 --> 00:42:22,170
and that you can do
that while still trying

777
00:42:22,170 --> 00:42:26,170
to maybe approximately get
monotonic improvement--

778
00:42:26,170 --> 00:42:28,450
PPO does not guarantee
monotonic improvement,

779
00:42:28,450 --> 00:42:30,330
but it can be pretty close--

780
00:42:30,330 --> 00:42:33,540
by thinking explicitly
about these lower bounds,

781
00:42:33,540 --> 00:42:35,970
and how much your
performance might change,

782
00:42:35,970 --> 00:42:38,220
and how much essentially
your state distribution might

783
00:42:38,220 --> 00:42:40,290
change, so that when
you're not confident

784
00:42:40,290 --> 00:42:42,300
in these approximations.

785
00:42:42,300 --> 00:42:45,580
It also uses generalized
advantage estimation,

786
00:42:45,580 --> 00:42:46,630
which can be helpful.

787
00:42:46,630 --> 00:42:49,450
And as I mentioned before,
it's extremely popular.

788
00:42:49,450 --> 00:42:51,490
You can use it in
many, many places,

789
00:42:51,490 --> 00:42:53,580
in part, because also you
don't need your reward

790
00:42:53,580 --> 00:42:55,440
function to be differentiable.

791
00:42:55,440 --> 00:42:57,200
So people have used
it in lots of domains.

792
00:43:01,423 --> 00:43:03,090
And the other thing
that I think is just

793
00:43:03,090 --> 00:43:05,730
useful to remember when we
think about policy gradients is

794
00:43:05,730 --> 00:43:08,710
that you can also use them
with actor-critic methods.

795
00:43:08,710 --> 00:43:12,300
So you can have
deep neural networks

796
00:43:12,300 --> 00:43:13,980
to approximate your
value function,

797
00:43:13,980 --> 00:43:16,350
and then use that for
your advantage estimation

798
00:43:16,350 --> 00:43:17,073
and combine them.

799
00:43:17,073 --> 00:43:18,490
And so that's what
most people do,

800
00:43:18,490 --> 00:43:21,480
is that they have some sort
of critic, a.k.a., your value

801
00:43:21,480 --> 00:43:24,210
function estimate, and a policy.

802
00:43:24,210 --> 00:43:26,580
And these are only to--

803
00:43:26,580 --> 00:43:29,250
reinforce and PPO are, of
course, not the only policy

804
00:43:29,250 --> 00:43:32,550
gradient algorithms, but they
are the backbone to-- well,

805
00:43:32,550 --> 00:43:34,180
they're still used
empirically a lot.

806
00:43:34,180 --> 00:43:36,300
And then also
they're the backbone

807
00:43:36,300 --> 00:43:37,390
to many of the other ones.

808
00:43:37,390 --> 00:43:38,890
So if you read other
papers, they'll

809
00:43:38,890 --> 00:43:40,800
be really useful baselines
that you often see

810
00:43:40,800 --> 00:43:43,670
or that people are building on.

811
00:43:43,670 --> 00:43:46,230
All right, we're now going to
go into imitation learning.

812
00:43:46,230 --> 00:43:48,647
But does anybody have any
questions before we start there?

813
00:43:54,040 --> 00:43:55,240
Yeah.

814
00:43:55,240 --> 00:44:00,060
On slide 22, just a general
question-- or, I guess,

815
00:44:00,060 --> 00:44:03,460
sorry, the one before this.

816
00:44:03,460 --> 00:44:08,380
Yeah, so this-- does that mean
when the policy is more myopic

817
00:44:08,380 --> 00:44:15,770
and gamma near 0, then your
step size will be like--

818
00:44:15,770 --> 00:44:21,400
you'll be able to improve
more to a greater extent?

819
00:44:21,400 --> 00:44:22,940
Yeah, that's a great question.

820
00:44:22,940 --> 00:44:25,790
So like, is the converse good?

821
00:44:25,790 --> 00:44:29,880
So if gamma is near
0, is this practical?

822
00:44:29,880 --> 00:44:31,720
I don't actually know
off the top of my head

823
00:44:31,720 --> 00:44:36,530
what C looks like for gamma
equals 0 or not 0, but near 0.

824
00:44:36,530 --> 00:44:40,790
So I don't think anybody
uses this in practice.

825
00:44:40,790 --> 00:44:43,850
I think they always use the
clipping or the KL trust region.

826
00:44:43,850 --> 00:44:46,190
So my guess is that it's
still not practical.

827
00:44:46,190 --> 00:44:50,750
Oftentimes, the C constants will
often be a function of V max,

828
00:44:50,750 --> 00:44:56,510
like your maximum value, often
scaled by 1 over 1 minus gamma.

829
00:44:56,510 --> 00:45:01,490
So it can really be quite
enormous in many cases.

830
00:45:01,490 --> 00:45:03,728
So it might be that here
it was particularly--

831
00:45:03,728 --> 00:45:06,020
they might be interested in
cases where your horizon is

832
00:45:06,020 --> 00:45:07,500
pretty large or where you--

833
00:45:07,500 --> 00:45:09,380
I think one thing here,
too, is that if we're

834
00:45:09,380 --> 00:45:11,668
in the episodic case, there's
not really a good reason

835
00:45:11,668 --> 00:45:13,460
to think that the
discount factor shouldn't

836
00:45:13,460 --> 00:45:16,280
be near 1, because you probably
actually do just care about all

837
00:45:16,280 --> 00:45:16,908
the rewards.

838
00:45:16,908 --> 00:45:18,950
So they're probably mostly
interested in domains,

839
00:45:18,950 --> 00:45:20,745
where they didn't think
it was reasonable.

840
00:45:20,745 --> 00:45:22,120
But yeah, that's
a good question.

841
00:45:26,780 --> 00:45:30,560
All right, let's talk
about imitation learning.

842
00:45:30,560 --> 00:45:34,320
So as we've said before, in
general, in computer science,

843
00:45:34,320 --> 00:45:36,183
we like to try to
reduce things if we can.

844
00:45:36,183 --> 00:45:37,850
We like to reduce
them to other problems

845
00:45:37,850 --> 00:45:38,933
that we know how to solve.

846
00:45:38,933 --> 00:45:40,580
And so imitation
learning is going

847
00:45:40,580 --> 00:45:43,920
to be our attempt to try to do
that, at least in certain ways,

848
00:45:43,920 --> 00:45:47,577
for all of
reinforcement learning.

849
00:45:47,577 --> 00:45:49,910
And some of these slides come
from some of my colleagues

850
00:45:49,910 --> 00:45:52,760
at Berkeley and at CMU.

851
00:45:52,760 --> 00:45:55,370
So in general, we're going to
now be thinking about the case

852
00:45:55,370 --> 00:45:57,600
where we're not going to
be gathering data online.

853
00:45:57,600 --> 00:46:01,160
So we saw in PPO that we tried
to reuse our data little bit

854
00:46:01,160 --> 00:46:02,773
more to take bigger steps.

855
00:46:02,773 --> 00:46:04,440
But one thing you
might wonder is, well,

856
00:46:04,440 --> 00:46:05,898
why do I need any
more data at all?

857
00:46:05,898 --> 00:46:08,510
Couldn't I just gather some
data, and then just use that?

858
00:46:08,510 --> 00:46:11,600
And maybe I don't need to
gather any new online data.

859
00:46:11,600 --> 00:46:15,770
And we'll see more ideas
about that shortly.

860
00:46:15,770 --> 00:46:18,440
But one case where you might
think that would be reasonable

861
00:46:18,440 --> 00:46:21,020
is, what if you have
great demonstrations?

862
00:46:21,020 --> 00:46:24,800
So you have instances of doctors
making really good decisions

863
00:46:24,800 --> 00:46:27,920
in the intensive care unit, or
you have people flying planes,

864
00:46:27,920 --> 00:46:29,860
or you have people driving cars.

865
00:46:29,860 --> 00:46:33,530
Why couldn't we just use those
examples to directly learn

866
00:46:33,530 --> 00:46:36,081
decision policies?

867
00:46:36,081 --> 00:46:38,240
And so the hope would
be there is if we just

868
00:46:38,240 --> 00:46:41,270
have those recordings, any time
someone's driving like a Tesla

869
00:46:41,270 --> 00:46:44,070
or someone's
driving an airplane,

870
00:46:44,070 --> 00:46:49,740
could we just get those sort of
state action pairs and tuples

871
00:46:49,740 --> 00:46:53,340
and use that information to
try to learn a policy directly?

872
00:46:53,340 --> 00:46:56,220
Now, one thing you
could do instead

873
00:46:56,220 --> 00:46:58,433
is to say like, well, you'd
have a human in the loop,

874
00:46:58,433 --> 00:47:00,100
but that's going to
be pretty expensive.

875
00:47:00,100 --> 00:47:02,910
And so the hope would be
that instead we could just

876
00:47:02,910 --> 00:47:05,080
use the demonstrations
people are already doing

877
00:47:05,080 --> 00:47:07,240
and that might be much
more reasonable, too,

878
00:47:07,240 --> 00:47:08,450
in terms of people's time.

879
00:47:11,915 --> 00:47:15,345
So one thing in
this case would be,

880
00:47:15,345 --> 00:47:16,720
all right, now,
maybe we're going

881
00:47:16,720 --> 00:47:18,460
to try to just look
directly at demonstrations,

882
00:47:18,460 --> 00:47:20,740
and that means we're not
going to need to have anybody

883
00:47:20,740 --> 00:47:21,590
to label things.

884
00:47:21,590 --> 00:47:25,960
This is an example from trying
to understand what the reward

885
00:47:25,960 --> 00:47:28,168
function might be for driving.

886
00:47:28,168 --> 00:47:30,210
So I guess, I should say,
in addition to the fact

887
00:47:30,210 --> 00:47:32,640
that we often have
data about people doing

888
00:47:32,640 --> 00:47:35,132
these sorts of complex tasks
that we'd like to imitate,

889
00:47:35,132 --> 00:47:36,840
it also might be in
those tasks that it's

890
00:47:36,840 --> 00:47:39,540
really hard for someone to
write down a reward function.

891
00:47:39,540 --> 00:47:41,740
Like, maybe in this
sorts of setting,

892
00:47:41,740 --> 00:47:44,193
you want to avoid the
water unless it's really,

893
00:47:44,193 --> 00:47:46,110
really steep or really
gravelly, in which case

894
00:47:46,110 --> 00:47:48,910
maybe your truck or a train
can go into the water.

895
00:47:48,910 --> 00:47:51,190
Or maybe like in general,
you want to avoid trees.

896
00:47:51,190 --> 00:47:54,270
But again, if it's really slippy
and muddy, it's actually better.

897
00:47:54,270 --> 00:47:56,640
And so it might just be that
it's really hard for people

898
00:47:56,640 --> 00:47:58,660
to write down a reward
function in this case.

899
00:47:58,660 --> 00:48:01,710
But they could drive it and
indicate that implicit reward

900
00:48:01,710 --> 00:48:02,530
function.

901
00:48:02,530 --> 00:48:06,900
And so again, that might
be easier to gather.

902
00:48:06,900 --> 00:48:08,650
This comes up in a lot
of different cases.

903
00:48:08,650 --> 00:48:10,650
And people have thought
about it certainly a lot

904
00:48:10,650 --> 00:48:15,220
for manipulating heavy machinery
or manipulating cars or things

905
00:48:15,220 --> 00:48:16,430
like that.

906
00:48:16,430 --> 00:48:20,900
But for things like driving
and parking and stuff,

907
00:48:20,900 --> 00:48:22,840
those are a lot of cases
where people provide

908
00:48:22,840 --> 00:48:24,100
those sorts of
demonstrations where

909
00:48:24,100 --> 00:48:26,205
it might be hard to specify
that reward function.

910
00:48:29,350 --> 00:48:32,410
So the idea from learning
from demonstrations

911
00:48:32,410 --> 00:48:36,560
is that you're going to get a
number of expert demonstrations.

912
00:48:36,560 --> 00:48:38,872
So experts will
demonstrate things,

913
00:48:38,872 --> 00:48:40,330
whether they're
flying a helicopter

914
00:48:40,330 --> 00:48:43,780
or manipulating something
with a robotic arm or stuff

915
00:48:43,780 --> 00:48:45,290
like through teleoperation.

916
00:48:45,290 --> 00:48:47,200
Dorsa Sadigh's group
does a lot of this.

917
00:48:47,200 --> 00:48:49,910
And it will give you a sequence
of states and actions--

918
00:48:49,910 --> 00:48:51,140
not rewards.

919
00:48:51,140 --> 00:48:57,065
So you just are going to have
trajectories of state action

920
00:48:57,065 --> 00:48:57,565
s-prime.

921
00:49:00,440 --> 00:49:02,480
So we're not going to
have any rewards anymore.

922
00:49:02,480 --> 00:49:06,500
Everything's just going to
be implicit in this case.

923
00:49:06,500 --> 00:49:10,370
And now, we're going to assume
that it's easier for people

924
00:49:10,370 --> 00:49:11,330
to do this.

925
00:49:11,330 --> 00:49:12,860
So they're just
going to, hopefully,

926
00:49:12,860 --> 00:49:14,570
be able to provide
these demonstrations

927
00:49:14,570 --> 00:49:17,530
or they maybe already have.

928
00:49:17,530 --> 00:49:20,082
So what's the setup
for the rest of today?

929
00:49:20,082 --> 00:49:22,540
The setup is that we still have
a state space and an action

930
00:49:22,540 --> 00:49:23,110
space.

931
00:49:23,110 --> 00:49:25,990
We're going to assume there's
some transition model.

932
00:49:25,990 --> 00:49:29,840
And there's a reward function,
but we don't know it.

933
00:49:29,840 --> 00:49:32,760
So there might be a reward
function, but we don't know.

934
00:49:32,760 --> 00:49:34,660
So there's nothing
explicit here.

935
00:49:34,660 --> 00:49:36,460
There's no explicit rewards.

936
00:49:36,460 --> 00:49:39,908
And that we have these
set of demonstrations.

937
00:49:39,908 --> 00:49:41,700
In behavior cloning,
what we're going to do

938
00:49:41,700 --> 00:49:43,470
is just reduce this
to supervised learning

939
00:49:43,470 --> 00:49:47,030
and try to learn a mapping
from states to actions.

940
00:49:47,030 --> 00:49:50,220
I'm just going to try
to clone the behavior.

941
00:49:50,220 --> 00:49:53,550
And then, we're going to also
see some about, can we actually

942
00:49:53,550 --> 00:49:56,400
recover the reward function
that people might be using

943
00:49:56,400 --> 00:49:58,180
to generate their behavior?

944
00:49:58,180 --> 00:50:00,060
And then if we have
that, can we actually

945
00:50:00,060 --> 00:50:03,240
try to get a new
good decision policy?

946
00:50:03,240 --> 00:50:07,016
But the first one is just to
try to directly learn a policy.

947
00:50:07,016 --> 00:50:10,320
So this is called
behavior cloning.

948
00:50:10,320 --> 00:50:13,320
And essentially, once
you decide to do this,

949
00:50:13,320 --> 00:50:15,960
this is just off-the-shelf
supervised learning.

950
00:50:15,960 --> 00:50:21,630
So now, you treat it as you have
a sequence of states and actions

951
00:50:21,630 --> 00:50:25,770
from your expert trajectories.

952
00:50:25,770 --> 00:50:27,840
And you can use whatever
tools and supervised

953
00:50:27,840 --> 00:50:29,860
learning you want.

954
00:50:29,860 --> 00:50:32,770
So just anything
can be done there.

955
00:50:32,770 --> 00:50:33,700
It's just to reduce.

956
00:50:33,700 --> 00:50:37,540
This is strictly now made into
a supervised learning problem.

957
00:50:37,540 --> 00:50:40,250
And there were some
really early successes.

958
00:50:40,250 --> 00:50:43,697
So like ALVINN from a very long
time ago, and then in 1993,

959
00:50:43,697 --> 00:50:45,280
learning to fly in
a flight simulator,

960
00:50:45,280 --> 00:50:48,010
really early on in the history
of reinforcement learning

961
00:50:48,010 --> 00:50:50,290
or the modern history of
reinforcement learning,

962
00:50:50,290 --> 00:50:52,782
people thought like, could
we just reduce this problem?

963
00:50:52,782 --> 00:50:55,240
And we'll see in a second what's
one of the challenges that

964
00:50:55,240 --> 00:50:57,010
comes up when we do this.

965
00:50:57,010 --> 00:50:58,760
But it certainly can
be really helpful.

966
00:50:58,760 --> 00:51:00,343
So it's kind of fun
to look at ALVINN.

967
00:51:00,343 --> 00:51:03,030
This was-- yeah, late '80s.

968
00:51:03,030 --> 00:51:05,030
But I think this must
have been kind of amazing.

969
00:51:05,030 --> 00:51:07,160
They were already
thinking about cars then.

970
00:51:07,160 --> 00:51:09,725
They're already thinking about
not-so-deep neural networks,

971
00:51:09,725 --> 00:51:11,600
but they were thinking
about neural networks.

972
00:51:11,600 --> 00:51:13,683
I think this came out of
CMU, if I remember right.

973
00:51:13,683 --> 00:51:17,750
And they had this tiny
30-by-32 video input.

974
00:51:17,750 --> 00:51:19,900
And they used this rangefinder.

975
00:51:19,900 --> 00:51:23,770
And so they were trying to use
not-so-deep neural networks

976
00:51:23,770 --> 00:51:26,920
to do behavior cloning for
driving in the late '80s, which

977
00:51:26,920 --> 00:51:29,616
is pretty awesome.

978
00:51:29,616 --> 00:51:34,063
So it can be done pretty well.

979
00:51:34,063 --> 00:51:36,480
In reality, this is something
that still people try a lot.

980
00:51:36,480 --> 00:51:39,970
It's a really good baseline
to try if you have good data.

981
00:51:39,970 --> 00:51:41,720
And I'll talk about
some of the challenges

982
00:51:41,720 --> 00:51:43,110
with doing behavior cloning.

983
00:51:43,110 --> 00:51:45,927
But I think one thing now is
like if you have a lot of data,

984
00:51:45,927 --> 00:51:47,760
like a lot, a lot, a
lot of demonstrations--

985
00:51:47,760 --> 00:51:50,253
like imagine you have all
the data from all the pilots,

986
00:51:50,253 --> 00:51:52,170
like you have their
actual what they're doing,

987
00:51:52,170 --> 00:51:54,720
all of the different sort of
input actions they're doing,

988
00:51:54,720 --> 00:51:56,803
and you have that for, I
don't know, all of United

989
00:51:56,803 --> 00:51:57,780
or something like that.

990
00:51:57,780 --> 00:52:00,230
So if you have an
enormous amount of data

991
00:52:00,230 --> 00:52:03,805
and you have a
pretty sophisticated

992
00:52:03,805 --> 00:52:05,180
supervised learning
technique, it

993
00:52:05,180 --> 00:52:07,340
can work really
well, particularly

994
00:52:07,340 --> 00:52:10,730
if you use behavioral cloning
with an RNN or something that

995
00:52:10,730 --> 00:52:12,650
takes a track of the history.

996
00:52:12,650 --> 00:52:17,360
So while what I wrote here
involved just states, and then

997
00:52:17,360 --> 00:52:19,380
like the last state,
like a Markov assumption,

998
00:52:19,380 --> 00:52:21,755
like the state and the action,
you don't have to do that.

999
00:52:21,755 --> 00:52:26,030
You could also say I could
have my state, action, state,

1000
00:52:26,030 --> 00:52:32,970
and then go from there to a1,
or state, action, state, action,

1001
00:52:32,970 --> 00:52:33,470
state.

1002
00:52:37,570 --> 00:52:39,250
So you, in general,
could use something

1003
00:52:39,250 --> 00:52:42,760
that is like a recurrent neural
network or anything that keeps

1004
00:52:42,760 --> 00:52:44,270
track of long term histories.

1005
00:52:44,270 --> 00:52:46,850
It does not have to be
a Markov representation.

1006
00:52:46,850 --> 00:52:50,140
And that often can
work very well.

1007
00:52:50,140 --> 00:52:51,910
Again, it depends a
lot on your domain.

1008
00:52:51,910 --> 00:52:55,430
I think that there's a nice
paper a few years ago in CORL,

1009
00:52:55,430 --> 00:52:58,300
which is one of the robotics
learning conferences, where they

1010
00:52:58,300 --> 00:53:00,970
looked at what were some of the
important factors when you're

1011
00:53:00,970 --> 00:53:06,370
doing offline learning and
for robot applications.

1012
00:53:06,370 --> 00:53:09,710
So it doesn't always work well,
but it can work really well,

1013
00:53:09,710 --> 00:53:12,310
particularly if you
use the history.

1014
00:53:12,310 --> 00:53:16,200
What domains might have
a history [INAUDIBLE]?

1015
00:53:16,200 --> 00:53:20,015
Because imagine if you're flying
