1032
00:53:50,350 --> 00:53:54,340
But in other cases, if it's
just your immediate sensors,

1033
00:53:54,340 --> 00:53:57,070
then you want the longer
history to capture that.

1034
00:53:57,070 --> 00:54:00,240
And same for planes and stuff.

1035
00:54:00,240 --> 00:54:01,920
Yeah, it's a good question.

1036
00:54:01,920 --> 00:54:04,180
So this is always just a
really good thing to try.

1037
00:54:04,180 --> 00:54:05,505
It's a really natural baseline.

1038
00:54:05,505 --> 00:54:06,880
It's generally
really easy to do.

1039
00:54:06,880 --> 00:54:09,886
People often report it
in offline [INAUDIBLE].

1040
00:54:09,886 --> 00:54:11,290
It's extensively used.

1041
00:54:11,290 --> 00:54:12,410
It does not always work.

1042
00:54:12,410 --> 00:54:15,170
Let's see why it might not work.

1043
00:54:15,170 --> 00:54:17,880
And I think one of the
themes that you're seeing now

1044
00:54:17,880 --> 00:54:20,070
with the policy
gradient work right now

1045
00:54:20,070 --> 00:54:22,530
is this challenge of what
are the states you reach

1046
00:54:22,530 --> 00:54:25,540
and how, when you use
different policies,

1047
00:54:25,540 --> 00:54:27,930
you're going to end up
at different states.

1048
00:54:27,930 --> 00:54:29,440
In general, that's
the definition.

1049
00:54:29,440 --> 00:54:32,027
If your policies don't ever
reach any different states

1050
00:54:32,027 --> 00:54:33,610
and they never take
different actions,

1051
00:54:33,610 --> 00:54:34,660
they're the same policy.

1052
00:54:34,660 --> 00:54:37,380
They generate the
same trajectories.

1053
00:54:37,380 --> 00:54:40,990
So DAGGER was a paper in 2008.

1054
00:54:40,990 --> 00:54:42,840
I'm trying to remember.

1055
00:54:42,840 --> 00:54:48,510
It came out in the first decade
of the 2000s to try to address

1056
00:54:48,510 --> 00:54:52,000
some of the challenges
with behavioral cloning.

1057
00:54:52,000 --> 00:54:54,720
And I think what
they were noticing is

1058
00:54:54,720 --> 00:54:57,220
this challenge of if
you do behavior cloning,

1059
00:54:57,220 --> 00:54:59,570
sometimes things go badly.

1060
00:54:59,570 --> 00:55:02,380
And essentially, that's
because the decisions

1061
00:55:02,380 --> 00:55:07,360
that you make over time
can have cascading effects.

1062
00:55:07,360 --> 00:55:09,050
So let's see what
that might look like.

1063
00:55:09,050 --> 00:55:16,470
So if you do something like
supervised learning, you--

1064
00:55:16,470 --> 00:55:18,920
and this is what we do
when we would be reducing

1065
00:55:18,920 --> 00:55:20,447
our problem to imitation.

1066
00:55:20,447 --> 00:55:22,030
In behavior cloning,
we just reduce it

1067
00:55:22,030 --> 00:55:22,988
to supervised learning.

1068
00:55:22,988 --> 00:55:27,120
In general, we assume that our
pairs, our data points, our x,

1069
00:55:27,120 --> 00:55:29,930
y pairs in supervised
learning are IID.

1070
00:55:29,930 --> 00:55:32,340
So they're are Independent
and Identically Distributed.

1071
00:55:32,340 --> 00:55:34,040
And they're ignoring
temporal structure,

1072
00:55:34,040 --> 00:55:36,410
because they just assume
they're totally independent.

1073
00:55:36,410 --> 00:55:40,240
But in our case, they're
absolutely related.

1074
00:55:40,240 --> 00:55:43,010
In fact, if you assume a Markov
structure, then what happens

1075
00:55:43,010 --> 00:55:46,590
is you have s0, a0, s1.

1076
00:55:46,590 --> 00:55:48,710
And so whatever you
did here exactly

1077
00:55:48,710 --> 00:55:50,950
helps determine what is
the next state you do.

1078
00:55:50,950 --> 00:55:52,700
So they're not-- the
states are definitely

1079
00:55:52,700 --> 00:55:56,420
not independent at all of
your different time points.

1080
00:55:56,420 --> 00:55:58,040
So one of the
challenges with that

1081
00:55:58,040 --> 00:56:01,790
is that if you have
independent and time errors--

1082
00:56:01,790 --> 00:56:03,410
and generally,
that's not too bad.

1083
00:56:03,410 --> 00:56:05,827
And that's what most of our
supervised learning guarantees

1084
00:56:05,827 --> 00:56:08,210
are for, is that you assume
your data is all IID,

1085
00:56:08,210 --> 00:56:11,120
and then you can think
about how much sort

1086
00:56:11,120 --> 00:56:15,270
of error in your estimates,
what sort of error you get.

1087
00:56:15,270 --> 00:56:18,180
So in general, if you
have an error at time

1088
00:56:18,180 --> 00:56:20,730
t with probability less
than or equal to epsilon

1089
00:56:20,730 --> 00:56:23,670
and you have T
decisions-- so let's

1090
00:56:23,670 --> 00:56:26,870
assume we have T decisions,
then your expected

1091
00:56:26,870 --> 00:56:30,020
number of total errors, if all
your decisions are independent,

1092
00:56:30,020 --> 00:56:35,360
is just epsilon times T
because they're all IID.

1093
00:56:35,360 --> 00:56:37,910
And that's not too terrible.

1094
00:56:37,910 --> 00:56:41,690
But that's not what we
normally have in-- oh,

1095
00:56:41,690 --> 00:56:42,930
I see what happened there.

1096
00:56:42,930 --> 00:56:43,430
OK.

1097
00:56:45,808 --> 00:56:47,350
OK, let's think
about something else.

1098
00:56:47,350 --> 00:56:49,000
I'll add a different
picture later.

1099
00:56:49,000 --> 00:56:52,810
Let's think of a racetrack.

1100
00:56:52,810 --> 00:56:55,900
All right, so in this
case, you have a racetrack.

1101
00:56:55,900 --> 00:57:00,050
And your car is driving, except
for your supervised learning

1102
00:57:00,050 --> 00:57:02,167
thing isn't perfect and
so it makes a small error.

1103
00:57:02,167 --> 00:57:04,250
So what you actually do--
you should maybe-- maybe

1104
00:57:04,250 --> 00:57:07,500
you actually should
have went this way,

1105
00:57:07,500 --> 00:57:10,200
but you went to the black part.

1106
00:57:10,200 --> 00:57:13,650
And now, you, again, make
a little bit of an error.

1107
00:57:13,650 --> 00:57:17,010
And now, you're off the track.

1108
00:57:17,010 --> 00:57:19,050
And now, this is really
tricky, because you

1109
00:57:19,050 --> 00:57:21,330
may have almost no
data in the part

1110
00:57:21,330 --> 00:57:24,943
of-- because your humans never
decided to drive off the track.

1111
00:57:24,943 --> 00:57:26,610
And so now, you're
in part of the region

1112
00:57:26,610 --> 00:57:28,985
where you have very little
data and very little coverage,

1113
00:57:28,985 --> 00:57:30,870
and you're even more
likely to make mistakes.

1114
00:57:30,870 --> 00:57:32,370
And so what you can
see in this case

1115
00:57:32,370 --> 00:57:34,820
is that if you make
small mistakes early on,

1116
00:57:34,820 --> 00:57:37,320
those can compound and get you
into parts of the state space

1117
00:57:37,320 --> 00:57:39,150
where you have
even less coverage

1118
00:57:39,150 --> 00:57:41,630
and you generally have
even less accuracy.

1119
00:57:41,630 --> 00:57:45,220
And so in general, you can
actually do much worse.

1120
00:57:45,220 --> 00:57:47,860
And this is because you have
a data distribution mismatch.

1121
00:57:47,860 --> 00:57:51,340
If the policy that
you compute gives you

1122
00:57:51,340 --> 00:57:54,710
a different distribution
between train and test,

1123
00:57:54,710 --> 00:57:57,220
then you don't necessarily
have the same guarantees.

1124
00:57:57,220 --> 00:57:59,428
And we're going to get a
different distribution here,

1125
00:57:59,428 --> 00:58:03,340
because the policy we were
using to gather the data

1126
00:58:03,340 --> 00:58:06,180
is not exactly the same
as the policy we get now.

1127
00:58:06,180 --> 00:58:08,638
And we saw that in PPO, too--
that when the policy changed,

1128
00:58:08,638 --> 00:58:10,763
we're going to get to
different states and actions.

1129
00:58:10,763 --> 00:58:12,460
What's causing our
policy to change here

1130
00:58:12,460 --> 00:58:16,000
is the fact that we can't
perfectly imitate the expert.

1131
00:58:16,000 --> 00:58:17,890
OK, so let's just see
what that looks like.

1132
00:58:17,890 --> 00:58:21,680
So in this case, we had--

1133
00:58:21,680 --> 00:58:24,710
in our training set,
we had pi star, which

1134
00:58:24,710 --> 00:58:26,060
we assume to be our expert.

1135
00:58:26,060 --> 00:58:28,790
And we were generating
states from pi star.

1136
00:58:28,790 --> 00:58:32,390
In our test set, we
have learned a policy

1137
00:58:32,390 --> 00:58:35,120
by trying to match the
state action pairs we

1138
00:58:35,120 --> 00:58:36,603
saw in our training
set and we're

1139
00:58:36,603 --> 00:58:38,395
getting a different
distribution of states.

1140
00:58:41,254 --> 00:58:43,393
In general, this is
going to be different.

1141
00:58:43,393 --> 00:58:45,435
And we're going to get
worse errors in this case.

1142
00:58:48,030 --> 00:58:51,070
Sorry about the-- I see
what happened in this case.

1143
00:58:51,070 --> 00:58:52,620
So I'll just draw it.

1144
00:58:52,620 --> 00:58:54,360
So what can happen
in this case is let's

1145
00:58:54,360 --> 00:58:56,340
say this is the
error you make now,

1146
00:58:56,340 --> 00:59:00,070
and then you can
make another error.

1147
00:59:00,070 --> 00:59:02,730
And it keeps compounding.

1148
00:59:02,730 --> 00:59:07,200
So if you make an error at
time step t with probability E,

1149
00:59:07,200 --> 00:59:09,000
essentially what
can happen there

1150
00:59:09,000 --> 00:59:14,790
is that you may then make errors
on the remaining time steps.

1151
00:59:14,790 --> 00:59:17,780
So could cause you to get
into parts of the state action

1152
00:59:17,780 --> 00:59:21,900
space for which you
make lots of errors,

1153
00:59:21,900 --> 00:59:26,010
and then you incur lots of
regret or costs through the end.

1154
00:59:26,010 --> 00:59:28,950
So in general, and I'm not going
to step through all of the proof

1155
00:59:28,950 --> 00:59:32,000
today, the error can actually
compound instead of linearly

1156
00:59:32,000 --> 00:59:33,250
with the number of time steps.

1157
00:59:33,250 --> 00:59:35,810
It can compound
quadratically, which

1158
00:59:35,810 --> 00:59:37,560
means that essentially
your performance is

1159
00:59:37,560 --> 00:59:40,020
much worse than supervised
learning would predict.

1160
00:59:40,020 --> 00:59:41,520
Supervised learning
said, oh, I've

1161
00:59:41,520 --> 00:59:46,210
got an epsilon optimal or
epsilon accurate policy.

1162
00:59:46,210 --> 00:59:46,950
Great.

1163
00:59:46,950 --> 00:59:49,860
And what this says is because
all of those decisions

1164
00:59:49,860 --> 00:59:52,070
are being made across
an entire trajectory,

1165
00:59:52,070 --> 00:59:53,820
you can actually end
up with epsilon times

1166
00:59:53,820 --> 00:59:57,290
T squared errors instead
of epsilon times T.

1167
00:59:57,290 --> 01:00:01,170
So this is what
motivated DAGGER.

1168
01:00:01,170 --> 01:00:02,760
DAGGER said, OK,
what's the problem?

1169
01:00:02,760 --> 01:00:05,240
The problem that's happening
here that we'd like to address

1170
01:00:05,240 --> 01:00:08,150
is whenever we make mistakes,
we go into a different part

1171
01:00:08,150 --> 01:00:09,140
of the state space.

1172
01:00:09,140 --> 01:00:11,570
Once we're there, we maybe
have very little guarantees

1173
01:00:11,570 --> 01:00:13,380
that we're going to do
anything reasonable.

1174
01:00:13,380 --> 01:00:15,320
So essentially, what
we want to try to do

1175
01:00:15,320 --> 01:00:18,740
is figure out how we
might correct or adjust

1176
01:00:18,740 --> 01:00:20,960
to those states that
we reach that weren't

1177
01:00:20,960 --> 01:00:23,120
in our original training set.

1178
01:00:23,120 --> 01:00:25,970
So the idea in this
case is that your-- this

1179
01:00:25,970 --> 01:00:27,960
is going to be an
iterative approach.

1180
01:00:27,960 --> 01:00:32,360
So you get a data set where
you take a current policy

1181
01:00:32,360 --> 01:00:34,430
and you execute it
in the environment.

1182
01:00:34,430 --> 01:00:37,940
So it's like you drive your
race car around a track.

1183
01:00:37,940 --> 01:00:41,270
And hopefully, it's similar
to what the expert would have

1184
01:00:41,270 --> 01:00:42,713
done, but probably not perfect.

1185
01:00:42,713 --> 01:00:44,630
And then what you do is
you go to your expert,

1186
01:00:44,630 --> 01:00:46,100
and you say, OK,
this is what I did

1187
01:00:46,100 --> 01:00:47,280
when I went around that track.

1188
01:00:47,280 --> 01:00:48,480
What should I have done there?

1189
01:00:48,480 --> 01:00:49,375
They're like a coach.

1190
01:00:49,375 --> 01:00:51,500
And so then what the coach
does or the expert does,

1191
01:00:51,500 --> 01:00:53,340
they say, ah, in
each of those states,

1192
01:00:53,340 --> 01:00:55,630
this is what you
should have done.

1193
01:00:55,630 --> 01:01:00,060
So it would say, hey,
if you went like this,

1194
01:01:00,060 --> 01:01:03,300
and then you did this and did
all these other crazy things

1195
01:01:03,300 --> 01:01:07,270
after that, it would have said,
OK, no, first of all, here,

1196
01:01:07,270 --> 01:01:08,620
you should have gone here.

1197
01:01:08,620 --> 01:01:10,110
And then once you
reached here, you

1198
01:01:10,110 --> 01:01:16,140
should have went down to try
to get back onto the road.

1199
01:01:16,140 --> 01:01:18,330
So essentially, what
you're having a human do is

1200
01:01:18,330 --> 01:01:20,347
you're having them label
at every time point,

1201
01:01:20,347 --> 01:01:21,930
at every state in
that trajectory what

1202
01:01:21,930 --> 01:01:23,670
they would have done.

1203
01:01:23,670 --> 01:01:27,270
And when you do that, that
gives you a new set of data

1204
01:01:27,270 --> 01:01:28,210
to learn from.

1205
01:01:28,210 --> 01:01:30,360
So it's like your
expert pilot gives you

1206
01:01:30,360 --> 01:01:32,640
feedback on every place
you made a mistake

1207
01:01:32,640 --> 01:01:34,950
when you just did
your last flight run,

1208
01:01:34,950 --> 01:01:36,430
and then you integrate that.

1209
01:01:36,430 --> 01:01:39,460
You're like, oh, OK, when I'm
feeling this form of lift,

1210
01:01:39,460 --> 01:01:40,660
next time, I got to do this.

1211
01:01:40,660 --> 01:01:42,960
So it gives you a
whole bunch more data,

1212
01:01:42,960 --> 01:01:45,970
and then we aggregate that data,
that's why it's called DAGGER.

1213
01:01:45,970 --> 01:01:49,620
So we're aggregating the data
sets of the old data we had

1214
01:01:49,620 --> 01:01:53,070
and the new data that we
just got from our expert.

1215
01:01:53,070 --> 01:01:55,830
We then do behavior
cloning again

1216
01:01:55,830 --> 01:01:58,020
on our new data set,
which now includes

1217
01:01:58,020 --> 01:02:01,340
more of the states
in the environment.

1218
01:02:01,340 --> 01:02:03,277
And then we repeat.

1219
01:02:03,277 --> 01:02:05,110
And I think part of the
motivation for this.

1220
01:02:05,110 --> 01:02:07,420
And this is why I said behavior
cloning can work really

1221
01:02:07,420 --> 01:02:10,180
well when you have enough data,
is that the problem that's

1222
01:02:10,180 --> 01:02:13,540
happening here is that we're
assuming we don't kind of have

1223
01:02:13,540 --> 01:02:15,010
full coverage over
the whole domain

1224
01:02:15,010 --> 01:02:17,910
of what the expert would do
at any place inside of the,

1225
01:02:17,910 --> 01:02:19,540
say, race car track.

1226
01:02:19,540 --> 01:02:21,490
