w determines our reward
function, this has to hold.

1470
01:16:25,790 --> 01:16:26,680
[INAUDIBLE]

1471
01:16:30,250 --> 01:16:33,880
And then what we can do is--

1472
01:16:33,880 --> 01:16:36,250
so it's sufficient
to say, well, what

1473
01:16:36,250 --> 01:16:38,530
would be one thing we could
do to be optimal if we

1474
01:16:38,530 --> 01:16:39,800
wanted to get to a policy?

1475
01:16:39,800 --> 01:16:43,970
Well, we just need to match
the features of the expert.

1476
01:16:43,970 --> 01:16:47,260
We need a policy that induces
a same distribution of states

1477
01:16:47,260 --> 01:16:49,060
as the expert.

1478
01:16:49,060 --> 01:16:53,860
So in general, if you have a
policy such that the features

1479
01:16:53,860 --> 01:16:57,070
you generate under that policy
are really close to the features

1480
01:16:57,070 --> 01:17:02,910
you get under pi star, then
for all w with w infinity

1481
01:17:02,910 --> 01:17:05,380
less equal to 1-- this is
using Holder's inequality.

1482
01:17:05,380 --> 01:17:08,520
You're guaranteed that
the reward of this policy

1483
01:17:08,520 --> 01:17:12,390
is very close to the reward
of the optimal policy

1484
01:17:12,390 --> 01:17:15,120
or your expert policy.

1485
01:17:15,120 --> 01:17:17,580
And all of this
is just to say you

1486
01:17:17,580 --> 01:17:21,750
can reduce the problem of
reward learning and policy

1487
01:17:21,750 --> 01:17:24,390
learning, in this case,
to feature matching.

1488
01:17:24,390 --> 01:17:26,470
That's kind of the high
level idea, is to say,

1489
01:17:26,470 --> 01:17:29,080
in the case where you don't
observe the reward directly,

1490
01:17:29,080 --> 01:17:32,650
but you have access to optimal
demonstrations by an expert,

1491
01:17:32,650 --> 01:17:36,330
all you need to do is to
find a policy and a reward

1492
01:17:36,330 --> 01:17:40,743
function that allows you
to match those features.

1493
01:17:40,743 --> 01:17:43,285
Because those are the features
that we know have high reward.

1494
01:17:47,531 --> 01:17:49,490
Now, as we've
already talked about,

1495
01:17:49,490 --> 01:17:51,730
there is still an infinite
number of reward functions

1496
01:17:51,730 --> 01:17:53,170
with the same optimal policy.

1497
01:17:53,170 --> 01:17:55,400
So even when we think about
this mapping to features,

1498
01:17:55,400 --> 01:17:59,080
it doesn't solve the
issue we just identified.

1499
01:17:59,080 --> 01:18:01,390
And there are many
stochastic policies that

1500
01:18:01,390 --> 01:18:03,800
can match the feature counts.

1501
01:18:03,800 --> 01:18:06,660
So I haven't told you anything
yet to solve that big problem.

1502
01:18:06,660 --> 01:18:09,170
I've just told you sort of
another way to think about it.

1503
01:18:09,170 --> 01:18:10,880
And so there's this
question of how

1504
01:18:10,880 --> 01:18:13,960
do we pick among all
these different options.

1505
01:18:13,960 --> 01:18:16,760
So there's a number of
different ways to do this.

1506
01:18:16,760 --> 01:18:20,540
Some of the largest and most
influential ideas are these two.

1507
01:18:20,540 --> 01:18:25,930
Maximum entropy inverse
reinforcement learning and GAIL.

1508
01:18:25,930 --> 01:18:28,000
And what we'll do
next time is to talk

1509
01:18:28,000 --> 01:18:30,070
about maximum entropy
inverse reinforcement

1510
01:18:30,070 --> 01:18:32,860
learning, which has been
very, very influential.

1511
01:18:32,860 --> 01:18:34,430
So this is in 2008.

1512
01:18:34,430 --> 01:18:36,890
So we'll pick up on that
on Thursday-- on Wednesday.

1513
01:18:36,890 --> 01:18:38,440
Thanks.

